WEBVTT

00:00:00.288 --> 00:00:02.748
Binary search tree search algorithms.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.500
You know, have you ever actually
stopped to wonder how a massive database

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:09.044
can pinpoint one specific
number out of literally

00:00:09.044 --> 00:00:11.120
millions in just a fraction of a second?

00:00:11.808 --> 00:00:15.148
Well, today, we're popping the hood on
a brilliantly efficient data structure

00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:17.468
that makes that exact
kind of speed possible.

00:00:18.048 --> 00:00:18.912
Let's get right into it.

00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:21.948
Here is our quick agenda for today.

00:00:21.948 --> 00:00:25.068
We're gonna kick things off with
a recap of trees and nodes,

00:00:25.068 --> 00:00:29.228
then break down the binary search tree
rule, map out the search algorithm itself,

00:00:29.228 --> 00:00:32.352
and finally lock it all
in with a worked example.

00:00:33.216 --> 00:00:35.876
Alright, section one.
Recap, Trees and Notes.

00:00:36.576 --> 00:00:40.956
So to lay the groundwork, let's look at
the basic anatomy of a standard tree.

00:00:40.956 --> 00:00:45.996
Think of a tree as basically a collection
of data points, which we call nodes,

00:00:45.996 --> 00:00:49.996
connected by paths or edges. Each
node holds a specific value.

00:00:50.688 --> 00:00:54.425
and the absolute golden rule
here, there is exactly one root

00:00:54.425 --> 00:00:57.788
node at the very top and
absolutely no loops anywhere.

00:00:57.788 --> 00:01:01.632
The data flows strictly downward,
kind of like a corporate org chart.

00:01:02.496 --> 00:01:06.616
Now if we restrict that structure
just a bit, we get a binary tree.

00:01:06.616 --> 00:01:10.356
Meaning each parent node branches
out to a maximum of two children.

00:01:10.356 --> 00:01:12.496
It's right there in the
name, right? Binary.

00:01:12.496 --> 00:01:16.656
A node can have zero, one, or two
children, but never more than two.

00:01:16.656 --> 00:01:20.160
And we keep it simple by just calling
them the left child and the right child.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:25.220
But here's the thing, a basic binary
tree is great for holding data, sure,

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:29.900
but it doesn't inherently help us find
that data quickly. It needs a spark.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:34.940
We need one specific, elegant rule to
transform it from a basic storage unit

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:36.660
into a high-speed search tool.

00:01:37.536 --> 00:01:40.636
which brings us to section 2,
the binary search tree rule.

00:01:41.472 --> 00:01:44.306
This rule is the secret sauce. Here it is.

00:01:44.306 --> 00:01:47.612
For every single node,
all the values in its left

00:01:47.612 --> 00:01:51.648
subtree are smaller, and all the value in
its right subtree is larger. If you look

00:01:52.032 --> 00:01:52.572
at the root

00:01:52.572 --> 00:01:57.243
node holding the number 8 right now,
check out its entire left side.

00:01:57.243 --> 00:01:58.892
Nodes 3, 1, and 6. Every

00:01:58.892 --> 00:02:00.652
single one of those is smaller than 8.

00:02:01.344 --> 00:02:06.684
now glance at the right side, the nodes
10, 9 and 14, all strictly larger than 8.

00:02:07.872 --> 00:02:10.724
And the absolute beauty of
this is that the rule applies

00:02:10.724 --> 00:02:13.932
universally across the entire
tree, not just at that top route.

00:02:14.496 --> 00:02:16.608
Take the number 3 on the
left side, for example.

00:02:16.956 --> 00:02:21.756
Its left child is 1, which is smaller.
Its right child is 6, which larger.

00:02:21.756 --> 00:02:23.836
Because of this totally
predictable sorting,

00:02:23.836 --> 00:02:26.836
you instantly know which
side a target has to be on.

00:02:26.836 --> 00:02:30.816
It guarantees you will never, ever need
to check every single value in the tree.

00:02:31.488 --> 00:02:34.588
Okay, moving to section
3, the search algorithm.

00:02:35.424 --> 00:02:38.880
Think of this algorithm like a wildly
efficient choose your own adventure map.

00:02:39.184 --> 00:02:42.944
You always start your search at the root.
Then you just compare your target value

00:02:42.944 --> 00:02:45.784
with the current node. If it's
a match, boom, you're done.

00:02:46.464 --> 00:02:49.404
If your target is smaller, you
move down to the left child.

00:02:49.404 --> 00:02:52.992
If it's larger, you moved to the right
child. You just repeat that loop.

00:02:53.304 --> 00:02:56.304
And if you ever need to move left or
right, but there's no node there,

00:02:56.304 --> 00:02:59.304
you immediately know your target
simply isn't in the tree.

00:03:00.096 --> 00:03:03.756
Let's actually see that in action.
Say we are searching for the number 9.

00:03:03.756 --> 00:03:08.436
We start at the root 8. 9 is
larger, so we go right. We hit 10.

00:03:08.436 --> 00:03:12.192
9 is smaller than 10, so go left.
And bam, there's our target.

00:03:12.672 --> 00:03:14.812
Look at how crazy efficient that is.

00:03:14.812 --> 00:03:17.692
Just by starting at eight
and realizing nine is larger,

00:03:17.692 --> 00:03:20.752
we instantly ignored the
entire left side of the tree.

00:03:20.752 --> 00:03:22.992
We didn't even glance at
the three, one, or six.

00:03:23.808 --> 00:03:27.448
Each step throws away a whole subtree,
so the search finishes quickly.

00:03:28.032 --> 00:03:31.512
That right there is why this data
structure is an absolute superpower.

00:03:31.512 --> 00:03:33.792
You aren't just crossing
off one number at a time.

00:03:33.792 --> 00:03:37.932
You are literally eliminating half of your
remaining work with a single comparison.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.472
Let's wrap this up with
section 4, a worked example.

00:03:42.144 --> 00:03:46.044
to really prove how bulletproof this
is. Let's do a side-by-side comparison.

00:03:46.044 --> 00:03:46.176
We're

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:50.044
going to search for a number that
does exist in R-Tree, the number 6,

00:03:50.044 --> 00:03:53.856
and pit it against a search for
number that doesn't exist, the 7.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.100
First up, tracking the successful search
for six. We start at the root, eight.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:00.960
Is six smaller or larger? Smaller,

00:04:01.344 --> 00:04:04.140
so we jump left. Now we're at three.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:05.856
Is six small or larger. Larger,

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:07.700
so branch right.

00:04:08.352 --> 00:04:11.772
We land on six, the value matches,
and mission accomplished.

00:04:11.772 --> 00:04:15.052
We found our target in exactly
three lightning-fast comparisons.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:19.340
Now, conversely, what
happens when we search for 7?

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:21.540
We follow that exact same initial path.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:26.540
Start at 8, go left to 3, 7 is
larger than 3, so we go right to 6.

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:31.940
Now we compare 7 with 6. 7 is bigger,
which means we absolutely must go right.

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:37.140
But look closely at the node for 6. It
has no right child. We've hit a dead end.

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:38.840
And because of that strict sorting rule,

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:41.940
we can definitively say
7 is not in this tree.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:44.140
We don't have to go check the
right side of the main tree at all.

00:04:44.544 --> 00:04:47.364
We know exactly where 7 should
be, and it's just not there.

00:04:48.288 --> 00:04:50.364
So we've seen how perfectly this algorithm

00:04:50.364 --> 00:04:52.588
hums along when the tree
branches out neatly.

00:04:53.568 --> 00:04:55.968
I want to leave you with a
bit of a curveball question.

00:04:56.328 --> 00:04:59.328
If a binary search tree cuts
our work in half at every step,

00:04:59.668 --> 00:05:02.708
what happens if the tree
becomes completely lopsided?

00:05:03.264 --> 00:05:07.864
Like, what if we added the numbers 1,
2, 3, 4, and 5 in that exact order?

00:05:07.864 --> 00:05:10.684
The tree would just be a single straight
line pointing down to the right.

00:05:11.424 --> 00:05:14.684
Would our superfast algorithms still
be able to throw away half the tree?

00:05:14.684 --> 00:05:16.604
Or would it completely break down?

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:18.624
Definitely something to chew on. Thanks

00:05:19.008 --> 00:05:20.640
so much for joining me on This Explaner,

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:21.792
and I'll catch you next time.
